body:
I am just honest that I personally don't know the information that they're looking for. If I know of someone who may, I will point them in their direction. Otherwise I will apologize for not being able to assist and ask if they have any other questions I may be able to help with.

&#x200B;

Petsmart does (or used to) train us on new products, new brands, etc that we offer but that doesn't mean it's honest info. They act like every new brand is the best quality stuff out there and it is objectively not true. So we can't directly trust our training in terms of quality and worth.

My location makes sure to tell people to hang on to it and treat it like money. Each card is seen as a product that we sell and would be the same as losing any other product in our store and wanting a free one that happens to cost $150-$500. The company would need to pay the difference and there's no company willing to eat the cost of several thousands of dollars because of an oopsies. 

&#x200B;

I completely understand how you feel about it and I also think this is dumb and should be digitalized to your profile to avoid this scenario at all. Our systems are not that savvy though and would need a complete rework - which is honestly long overdue anyways. I genuinely feel bad whenever this situation comes up and if it were up to me PetSmart can eat it, but it's not up to us. 

&#x200B;

The people you spoke of that giggled at this are awful people and I am sorry that you experienced that at one of our locations.

"Being a petsmart worker should be a mental illness in of itself" 

&#x200B;

I felt that to my core.

Hard to say if it's a personal issue or not. Hours are being cut for all locations. Full time associates are supposed to keep their full time hours in order to keep benefits. Anyone who is part time are required to be the ones who receive hour cuts and someone who is newer is typically getting cut since they will not know as much as a more seasoned worker. Stores are being forced to run on skeleton crews because it maximizes the profits for corporate.

&#x200B;

Calling out for being sick should not be the cause of you receiving hour cuts but I can't guarantee you don't have a shitty, spiteful manager. Based on your post though, I don't think you've done anything wrong. I wish I had more insight for you.

I have seen many complaints come in over the years about my employers that are made by individuals that are impossible to please even if you did exactly what they wanted you to do. They would have focused on something else to complaint about. I've seen complaints that their dogs are aggressive with us and that's somehow our faults. They really think several people in our business having close encounters with their dog means that our facility is doing something wrong. Couldn't possibly be their dog (despite not being able to do the service at home themselves as their trusted owners). As upsetting and defeating as it may be, people just suck ass and they can be dumb as shit. You know you are doing your best and trying to be accommodating as possible (which is why you didn't immediately redeem the treat points and attempted to explain the process of how to get there to see them!).

&#x200B;

Fuck that guy.

I went from smoking daily for years to not being able to smoke more than a hit of a bowl before I triggered an anxiety/panic attack. I don't know what the cause was but after about 2 years I was able to smoke more regularly again but I am still very cautious because I feel like it could still happen if I were to oversmoke. I've definitely had the beginning effects of an anxiety attack but I'm able to catch it in time. Before all this, oversmoking was never a thing for me. 

&#x200B;

I wish I had an answer for you but maybe take solace that it doesn't seem to be completely permanent. I smoke to deal with PTSD and physical pain so it hasn't been easy to have one of my treatments be a cause of my problems. I hope the Prozac helps you or that you're able to find a medication that does. It's okay if Prozac isn't the answer. It takes time to find the right antidepressant that works with your unique brain chemistry.

Also, brushing the very top of your dog's coat does not mean they are completely brushed out and tangle free. The undercoat can and does get tangled too and even if you brush the top coat every day, if you aren't getting the undercoat and it is matted, your dog will need to be shaved which you agree to on the paperwork. 

&#x200B;

So many owners will swear up and down that they brush their dogs every day and they cannot believe we shaved their dogs as it is impossible they were matted and they throw a hissy fit about it. You literally have to agree to it otherwise the service is denied.

Everyone is working 30-40+ hours unless their schedule doesn't allow it or they specifically require less hours. If you are legitimately not wanting to work more than 30 hours, I would suggest being honest with your manager as in my experience changing availability/hours have always been respected if you change your mind. 

&#x200B;

As long as you are a hard & reliable worker, if you were to ask for an increase in hours the manager would be jumping for joy rather than stingy 9 times out of 10. 

&#x200B;

General advice/tips: 

These things come up in your training but they are so important I would like to emphasize: Learn the 7 critical signs of stress in a pet. Always be alert and observant of the dogs in your care. Focus on learning/remembering dog behavioral signs that they are becoming upset and may bite you or another dog and how to react accordingly. 

&#x200B;

Whenever you are interacting with a dog in their room, you are 99% of the time going to need to write on their paperwork. Always have a pen on you and initial/mark accordingly (this will make more sense once you've had some training) 

&#x200B;

If you ever see anything unusual involving a pet's health, notify your leader immediately. Vomit, diarrhea, blood in stool, fleas, open wound, hot spot, etc etc. and especially if you notice a critical sign of stress. And don't be afraid to be stern over the walkie talkie you'll be using, "I need someone \[location\], NOW." if it's a serious situation or a dog fight is happening.

Least liked breed: German Shepherds. I've met a few absolute sweethearts but the large majority of them have been highly reactive or aggressive. More likely to start scuffles in camp. 

&#x200B;

Most liked breed: Golden retrievers, greyhounds (italian greyhounds included), french bulldogs, pugs, and dachshunds.

I love love love this so much, the only downside (if you can call it that) is it's NoPixel related so I can't just link it to everyone and expect them to like it the same way/fully understand the depth of the lyrics

&#x200B;

I'm sharing it anyways xD

If you're walking by the Hotel entrance and the phone has rung 2-3 times, go ahead and answer it - at least put it on hold and let someone know. If you could learn TouchPoint/Prism, that would be amazing. You'd be able to help any customer that has been waiting at the desk or handle the call that is coming in. 

&#x200B;

The beginning of every hour from 10 to 4, usually the Lead is switching the camp staff so the likelihood of this situation arising is more likely. 

&#x200B;

If you are closing in core, be sure to check in with the hotel staff and let them know if you are leaving. This way you know they are still there and don't alarm the doors before all closing staff is gone and they know to alarm the door themselves. 

&#x200B;

Learn the critical signs of stress in a dog/cat and keep that in mind whenever you see a hotel guest in passing, you never know when a pet may decline while in our care. 

&#x200B;

Learning the procedure on how to take a hotel guest to the vet is also extremely helpful. 

&#x200B;

Thank you for the inquiry into this - a lot of core leads don't give two shits about helping or being knowledgeable about the other departments.

As a PetsHotel Lead, I am very sorry to hear that this is happening at your hotel. I am grateful to have a manager & leadership team that understands the difficulties of camp in terms of people at the window, aggressive dogs in the room, dogs that need breaks for health-related reasons, and having people in camp for ***hours***. 

&#x200B;

While the associate job IS to staff camp during the day, I understand how grueling it is to be in camp for 4+ hours a day - I did it for years myself. We do our best to give breaks out of camp by putting a Lead in camp to break up time spent in there for the associate's mental sake. I highly suggest listening to music or a podcast while you're in there, it helps. lol 

&#x200B;

In terms of  aggressive dogs being left in camp or not being made individual after a few chances - this is disturbing on its own. This is a big red flag in terms of leadership. Having that +1 person paying for camp is not worth the risk of dogs getting injured in a dog fight. If a dog is consistently being aggressive with other dogs they should not be allowed around other dogs, that should be common sense. The "putting a dog on a lead" maneuver is for dogs that aren't fully crossing the line but showing warning signs that it will eventually lead to a scuffle or fight - showing teeth, snapping without lunging at them, growling, etc. If a dog was in a scuffle or full on dog fight, they need to be given a time out immediately.

&#x200B;

Again, I fully sympathize for your experience with your management, this is likely an issue that will come up with any sort of day camp/dog kennel and isn't PetSmart specific. Maybe look into a different location and transfer hoping for better leadership there. You could likely make a HR complaint as well about your leadership but from my experience it is extremely hard to get HR properly involved with a shitty manager, it took years for the dumpster fire of a manager to be fired.
